1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,279
LoreVia is an AI-powered manuscript intelligence platform for fiction authors.

2
00:00:04,499 --> 00:00:09,727
The engineering problem is maintaining character, plot, and continuity context across long drafts.

3
00:00:09,947 --> 00:00:18,470
The product combines a Next.js author workspace, a FastAPI application, PostgreSQL with pgvector, object storage, and Celery workers.

4
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:24,488
This walkthrough follows a manuscript from upload to evidence-backed analysis, then examines recovery and deployment.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,144
The Next.js App Router hosts the author workspace and interactive demo.

6
00:00:34,364 --> 00:00:41,292
React components own the editing interface; TanStack Query manages server state, while Zustand stores selected client state.

7
00:00:41,512 --> 00:00:45,355
Axios sends credentialed API requests through a same-origin rewrite.

8
00:00:45,575 --> 00:00:50,599
These screenshots use the repository's synthetic demo content, not customer manuscripts.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:57,743
FastAPI validates endpoint input with Pydantic schemas and resolves authenticated users before service calls.

10
00:00:57,963 --> 00:01:04,721
LoreVia issues access and refresh JWTs in HttpOnly cookies, with CSRF checks on unsafe requests.

11
00:01:04,941 --> 00:01:08,605
SQLAlchemy uses async sessions against PostgreSQL.

12
00:01:08,825 --> 00:01:13,148
Services own multi-step transactions; Alembic revisions evolve the schema.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,468
On upload, the API bounds and validates the file, previews structure, checks allowances, and rejects a duplicate active upload for the same user.

14
00:01:22,688 --> 00:01:30,876
The upload service writes the source to S3-compatible storage, commits the Book, processing run, and task execution, then dispatches after commit.

15
00:01:31,096 --> 00:01:34,539
The response reports processing while the long job continues.

16
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,109
Redis carries Celery messages.

17
00:01:47,329 --> 00:01:53,303
Five worker roles isolate extraction, analysis, embedding, intelligence, and maintenance.

18
00:01:53,523 --> 00:01:56,871
Extraction and analysis also consume priority queues.

19
00:01:57,091 --> 00:02:01,475
The launcher registers only the modules each role needs; Beat schedules maintenance.

20
00:02:01,695 --> 00:02:10,038
Late acknowledgments, one-message prefetch, task timeouts, and child recycling bound failure and memory behavior, but redelivery requires replay-safe tasks.

21
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,823
An extraction worker reads the stored manuscript, parses chapters, and creates chapter work.

22
00:02:20,043 --> 00:02:25,012
Chapter processing builds chunks, character candidates, identities, and relationship data.

23
00:02:25,232 --> 00:02:30,090
Analysis tasks derive summaries and narrative signals; embedding tasks create vectors.

24
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:34,754
The UI reports stored processing state, not a prediction from a browser timer.

25
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,688
Book chunks and character embeddings use 1,536-dimensional pgvector columns.

26
00:02:44,908 --> 00:02:53,031
Search embeds the question, queries book-scoped chunks, filters by chapter to respect spoiler boundaries, and can combine vector and lexical candidates.

27
00:02:53,251 --> 00:02:58,095
Advisor prompts use retrieved passages, and grounded answer schemas carry citations.

28
00:02:58,315 --> 00:03:02,884
Retrieval narrows context; it does not make model output automatically reliable.

29
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,819
Long-running work can fail after a database commit or during a broker publish.

30
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:15,177
LoreVia records executions and an outbox in PostgreSQL, then a maintenance task reconciles stalled dispatch.

31
00:03:15,397 --> 00:03:20,430
Workers claim rows under a lock and use stable execution identities; terminal results are guarded.

32
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:24,604
The tradeoff is more control-plane code, but it makes retries auditable.

33
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,043
One operational issue was memory spent importing every task module in every worker and in health probes.

34
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:42,807
Role-specific loading reduced idle imports.

35
00:03:43,027 --> 00:03:49,310
The health check now creates a minimal Celery client and pings the exact worker identity without loading SQLAlchemy or tasks.

36
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:54,874
Local Docker tests covered registration and failure states; VPS memory gains remain unmeasured.

37
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:12,733
The tracked deployment places the Next.js frontend on Vercel and routes API traffic through Cloudflare to Nginx and FastAPI on a Hetzner VPS.

38
00:04:12,953 --> 00:04:15,627
Compose runs Redis and the five worker roles.

39
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:24,235
Supabase supports OAuth, R2-compatible storage holds files, Sentry captures backend failures, and GitHub Actions defines test and image gates.

40
00:04:24,455 --> 00:04:29,973
The hosted product may be retired; the repository preserves the engineering design and verification history.
